      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the number of different settings for the suitcase lock where all three digits are different, we need to consider the choices for each digit and ensure that no digit is repeated.\n\n1. **Choosing the first digit:**\n   The first digit can be any of the 10 digits (0 through 9). Therefore, there are 10 possible choices for the first digit.\n\n2. **Choosing the second digit:**\n   The second digit must be different from the first digit. Since there are 10 digits in total and one of them has already been used, there are 9 remaining choices for the second digit.\n\n3. **Choosing the third digit:**\n   The third digit must be different from both the first and the second digits. Since two digits have already been used, there are 9 remaining choices for the third digit (it can be any of the 10 digits, excluding the two already used).\n\nNow, we multiply the number of choices for each digit to get the total number of different settings:\n\n\\[\n10 \\text{ (choices for the first digit)} \\times 9 \\text{ (choices for the second digit)} \\times 9 \\text{ (choices for the third digit)} = 10 \\times 9 \\times 9 = 810\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different settings possible is \\(\\boxed{810}\\).",
